WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.320
Lucie Fink: As a content creator

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.003
with my own business online,

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:04.950
I'm normally someone who works from home,

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.910
but ever since the coronavirus outbreak

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.630
and the call to stay indoors,

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:11.610
not only has the type of content
that I'm producing shifted,

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.240
but also my workflow has shifted.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.765
Ashley Ballard: Hey,
everybody. My name's Ashley.

00:00:15.765 --> 00:00:17.368
Lucie: Hey, everyone, I'm Lucie Fink.

00:00:17.368 --> 00:00:19.080
Coco Bassey: Hi, my name is Coco Bassey,

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.407
and I'm the founder and
creator of Millennielle.com.

00:00:21.407 --> 00:00:23.320
Ashley: And I'm a blogger
and social-media editor

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:24.380
based in New York City.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.380
Lucie: I'm a video producer
and lifestyle host,

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.255
and I run my own media company.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:30.920
Coco: Normally on a day like this...

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.310
Ashley: I'd be going to the office

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.650
or checking out my favorite coffee shop.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.440
Coco: I would be out
shooting content for brands,

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.500
maybe be taking a client out to lunch.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.900
Ashley: But instead,
I'm cooped up by myself

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.550
in my Brooklyn apartment.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.214
I've had a lot of privilege that

00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:47.770
a lot of other people don't
necessarily get to have,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.610
which is getting to work from home.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.120
Coco: It's definitely not
ideal for creatives like me

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.800
that do most of our job outdoors.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.460
Lucie: Often, I'm working
with a team of other people.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.240
If I'm doing a high-quality video shoot

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:00.360
outside of my apartment,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.330
sometimes I'll hire a
videographer or an audio expert.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.540
When it comes to videographers
and photographers,

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.930
I have totally had to do
all of that on my own.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.130
So a lot of what I'm shooting

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.180
is just setting up a camera on a tripod

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.260
or just leaning it against the windowsill

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.830
and shooting it myself.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:16.847
At the start of this,
it was really tempting

00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:19.770
to kind of just continue
creating content like normal,

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.045
but I've realized that right now

00:01:21.045 --> 00:01:23.186
the world is just not normal.

00:01:23.186 --> 00:01:25.510
And if you just avoid that
and don't talk about it,

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.470
people are not going to
find what you're saying

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.460
to be useful or relevant at all.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.510
Coco: So, creating content at home

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.480
doesn't always have to
be this huge production,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:33.590
and I think that's one of the things

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:37.370
that a lot of creators
kind of get stuck doing.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:41.010
Like, when we are unable to get outside

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:44.170
and create this, like,
amazing, extravagant setup,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.820
we think that we're, like,
unable to work at all,

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.470
and that's totally not true.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.295
I have my iPhone,

00:01:51.295 --> 00:01:55.211
my vlogging camera, which is a Canon G7 X,

00:01:55.211 --> 00:01:57.920
and then I also have my DSLR camera

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.700
that I tend to use for some
of my bigger brand projects.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.441
I will always make sure
that the camera is at my,

00:02:07.441 --> 00:02:09.340
basically at my, like, hip or waist level

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.820
because that's kind of where
it looks the most flattering.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.340
I don't like to go that much lower,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.765
otherwise it kind of
feels like the camera's

00:02:15.765 --> 00:02:17.614
looking up my nose [laughs],

00:02:17.614 --> 00:02:19.243
which is not that great either.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.238
Lucie: More and more
recently, I've found myself

00:02:22.238 --> 00:02:24.560
gravitating towards shooting on my iPhone.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.980
I don't have any of my
soft box lights with me,

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.560
so I've just been using windows

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.330
as a source of natural light.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.010
Ashley: I usually have a tripod,

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.730
but since I left it at my office

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.640
before the quarantine happened,

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.300
I've been using a stack of
books on top of a stool.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.250
This is A-OK if you don't own a tripod,

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:46.230
Like, you can totally improvise
with anything that you have.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.180
Personally, I'm focused on
doing video-based content

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.990
because that is a way for
me to diversify my work

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.560
and connect with people better.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.410
It also gives people more of an idea

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:56.950
of how I'm doing in my house

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.730
and, like, kind of what I'm working on.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.800
And it makes me feel more
connected to the outside world.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.770
Coco: So, when it comes
to experimental content,

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.230
I will definitely, without a doubt,

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.970
say that it's been getting into TikTok,

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.137
which is kind of crazy to think about.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.720
I never thought that
I would be the creator

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.320
that would get into TikToks.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.480
I just didn't think it
was a platform for me.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.620
But I've seen some really,
really cool content on there,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.540
and it's also kind of cool to play around

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.430
with video editing, which is something

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:35.360
I've always wanted to be better at.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.210
Lucie: I started using
TikTok just as a fun outlet

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.890
for me to dance, and it
actually makes me sweat a lot.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.060
And then people seemed
to like the TikTok stuff,

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.539
so I created a YouTube video tutorial

00:03:45.539 --> 00:03:47.880
showing them how to do a
TikTok dance that I love.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.530
I'm a savage.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:50.610
Huh.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:51.470
OK.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:52.389
That's how it goes.

00:03:52.389 --> 00:03:54.130
[laughing]

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:55.370
I'm not a dance instructor,

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.603
so I usually work with dance instructors.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.180
And I've also been experimenting

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.060
with some quarantine-specific content.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.630
So, cutting my husband's
hair in quarantine

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:06.400
and also my morning routine in quarantine.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.380
And people seem to be
resonating with that as well.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.290
Coco: Been doing a lot
more skin care and beauty.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:13.960
I feel like now is the
time to get out there

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.323
and experiment with some
new products and stuff

00:04:16.323 --> 00:04:18.475
and even play around with makeup,

00:04:18.475 --> 00:04:20.824
'cause if you mess it up,
no one's gonna see it.

00:04:20.824 --> 00:04:21.900
[laughs]

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:23.580
I feel like these times now

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.990
I have so much more time to
connect with my audience.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.643
Ashley: It's easier said
than done, depending on

00:04:29.643 --> 00:04:31.850
the size of your account,
but it's important

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.890
to be extra responsive to your audience.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.670
A lot of people are probably coming to you

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:39.373
from a really anxious and unsure place.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.040
Lucie: Digital connection is everything.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.968
It's one of the only
ways that people are able

00:04:44.968 --> 00:04:47.092
to stay connected with
their families and friends

00:04:47.092 --> 00:04:48.493
that they're not with right now.

00:04:48.493 --> 00:04:51.330
And, honestly, it's how people
are maintaining a lifeline

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.220
and a connection to the outside world.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.588
Ashley: My No. 1 tip is
just finding the tool

00:04:57.588 --> 00:05:00.189
that, like, gets you the
closest to your audience

00:05:00.189 --> 00:05:01.860
that makes you feel comfortable.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.100
One big thing that I've
been experimenting with more

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.370
is doing livestreaming

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.120
when I make my coffee in the morning

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:08.910
and I'm relaxing on my windowsill.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.010
I just turn on my live,

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.280
and I just ask people how they're feeling.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.255
Coco: I've been loving

00:05:15.255 --> 00:05:17.571
running polls on my Instagram stories.

00:05:17.571 --> 00:05:19.530
Lucie: Some of the ways that
I've connected with my audience

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.740
right now are by using the
sticker tab on Instagram.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.188
That's a great way to hear
directly from your audience

00:05:25.188 --> 00:05:27.300
in response to a targeted question.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.869
I use my YouTube
community tab pretty often

00:05:29.869 --> 00:05:32.760
as a way to ask questions
or to take polls.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.817
Ashley: A lot of people are
turning to content right now

00:05:35.817 --> 00:05:39.003
to just, you know, feel a
tiny ounce of normality.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:42.710
Lucie: And if you do have
a platform with an audience

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:44.970
that's listening to you,
just use it right now

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.043
to spread some sort of positivity.

